<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Kumamoto Domain</h1><p class="st-meta">1600 CE – 1871 CE · capital Kumamoto</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The domain of Higo in central Kyushu, ruled from Kumamoto's mighty castle by the Kato and then the Hosokawa family.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Kato Kiyomasa</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi's</a> leading generals, received northern Higo in 1588 and the whole province after supporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1600. He completed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> castle around 1607, one of the strongest fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and carried out major river and land reclamation works.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1632 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> removed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-kiyomasa">Kiyomasa</a>'s son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kato-tadahiro">Tadahiro</a> and gave Higo to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-tadatoshi">Hosokawa Tadatoshi</a> with 540,000 koku; the Hosokawa ruled until 1871. Tadatoshi hosted the swordsman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyamoto-musashi">Miyamoto Musashi</a> in his last years. In the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-shigekata">Hosokawa Shigekata</a> reformed finances and administration and founded the Jishukan school and a medical academy.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto">Kumamoto</a> took a cautious line in the Restoration and joined the new government. After abolition in 1871 it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumamoto-prefecture">Kumamoto Prefecture</a>, and in 1877 the castle withstood a siege by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigo Takamori's</a> rebels.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kumamoto-domain.jpg" alt="Kumamoto Domain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kumamoto_Castle_05n3200.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1600 CE – 1871 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kumamoto</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kato Kiyomasa, Hosokawa Tadatoshi, Hosokawa Shigekata</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Japanese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Higo Domain</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=kumamoto-domain" aria-label="Kumamoto Domain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8031 986 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kumamoto Domain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8351 1242L8530 1145M8351 1242L8529 1145"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8530 1145h0M8529 1145h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8351 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8351 1242h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kumamoto. <a href="/atlas?year=1736&amp;focus=kumamoto-domain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
